* Show in order to price a European put via FFT we just need to choose \(\alpha<-1\).
* In FFT, we define the range for log of strikes as \(k_{m}= eta+(m-1)\Delta k= eta+(m-1)\lambda\), for \(m=1,\ldots,N\) and some \( eta\). There are many choices for \( eta\). One of which is to set \( eta=\ln\,S_{0}- rac{\lambda N}{2}\). This choice for \( eta\) would cause at-the-money strike to fall in the middle of our range of strikes where \(S_{0}\) is today spot. For this choice of \( eta\), if we are interested in finding the premium for \(k=\log(K)\) we would typically interpolate. (a) If we are interested in finding the premium for a specific strike, say \(k_{0}=\log(K_{0})\) without any interpolation, what \( eta\) would we choose? Find the index for this \( eta\). (b) What \( eta\) would choose such that the first entry coincides to the premium for \(k_{0}\)?
